{% block title %}Bought for $1.2 million. Sold to Sagamok for $7 million. | RHT Circle{% endblock %}
{% block description %}Public records connect seller Paul Guindon's companies to Serpent River housing projects and a GR Truss financing record involving Sagamok adviser Ryan McLeod.{% endblock %}
{% block og_title %}Bought for $1.2 million. Sold to Sagamok for $7 million.{% endblock %}
{% block og_description %}The South Market land transaction and the public records connecting Paul Guindon's companies, Serpent River housing, Ryan McLeod and GR Truss.{% endblock %}

{% block article_body %}
  <p class="lead">Sagamok's project company paid $7 million for the land at 201 South Market Street in Sault Ste. Marie. The seller, a numbered company owned by Sault contractor Paul Guindon, had bought it fifteen months earlier for $1.2 million.</p>

  <p>Council had approved $7 million of Robinson-Huron Treaty settlement money for the project. Sagamok now describes South Market as a roughly $45-million development, with $7 million in equity and $38 million to be financed.</p>

  <p>Members have not been shown an independent appraisal, purchase agreement or due-diligence report explaining the $5.8-million increase.</p>

  <h2>Who is connected to what</h2>

  <p>Paul Guindon owns the company that sold Sagamok the South Market land. He is also an owner and director of RSG General Contracting and the sole active director of a second numbered company, 1890713 Ontario Inc. Serpent River and Elliot Lake records connect both numbered companies to RSG housing projects.</p>

  <p><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryan-mcleod-0b425516a">Ryan McLeod</a> is Sagamok's external financial adviser. He was also Serpent River's financial consultant. He presented Sagamok Council with its Sault housing update in March 2024 and presented Serpent River's Elliot Lake opportunity involving RSG two months later.</p>

  <p>GR Truss is a Garden River-based manufacturer of engineered roof and floor trusses. Sagamok acquired its operating company, 2262012 Ontario Limited, on April 1, 2025 through its real-estate corporate structure. Chief Angus Toulouse is one of its three directors and serves as President.</p>

  <p>About eleven weeks before Sagamok acquired GR Truss, McLeod's company and Guindon's company were registered together as secured parties over one of its payloaders.</p>

  <h2>Sagamok approved $7 million without naming the seller</h2>

  <p>The land was already owned by Guindon's company when Sagamok Council began putting Nation money into the project.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>August 8, 2023:</strong> Guindon's 1000602533 Ontario Inc. bought 201 South Market Street for $1.2 million.</li>
    <li><strong>March 7, 2024:</strong> McLeod presented Council with a project update. Council increased Sagamok's early allocation from $100,000 to $300,000 so construction could continue while CMHC approval was pending. The minutes say the money was requested by "they" but do not identify who that means.</li>
    <li><strong>May 28, 2024:</strong> Council approved an investment partnership and a BCR for the CMHC development. The posted minutes do not include the presentation or agreement.</li>
    <li><strong>October 8, 2024:</strong> Council approved $7 million from the Treaty settlement for the project. The motion does not name the seller or developer or record an appraisal, rate of return, security or repayment term.</li>
    <li><strong>November 13, 2024:</strong> Guindon's company sold the land for $7 million to the company named on title for Sagamok's project.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The $300,000 and $7-million approvals are separate. The posted record does not say whether the earlier allocation was additional, repaid or included in the $7 million.</p>

  <p>Sagamok's 2025 finance presentation describes a roughly $45-million development with three 40-unit apartment buildings. It lists $7 million in equity, $38 million to be financed and projected annual generation of $250,000 to $350,000.</p>

  <p>In May 2026, Council approved a CMHC loan guarantee for South Market. The same record says the CMHC commitment was not yet finished and a Build Canada Homes application remained under final review.</p>

  <p>Members still have not been shown the appraisal, purchase agreement, development agreement, final financing terms or current construction contract.</p>

  <h2>Serpent River worked with the same companies, then reviewed both projects</h2>

  <p>Serpent River had two urban housing projects involving Guindon's companies and McLeod: a 151-unit development in Sault Ste. Marie and a 22-townhouse development in Elliot Lake.</p>

  <h3>Sault Ste. Marie</h3>

  <p>Serpent River's March 12, 2024 minutes name 1890713 Ontario Inc., identified as RSG General Contracting, as the constructor. Council authorized a phased loan of up to $5 million toward the project, then estimated at $57.5 million.</p>

  <h3>Elliot Lake</h3>

  <p>A City report identifies Guindon's 1000602533 Ontario Inc. as the original buyer of 80 Dieppe Avenue and calls it RSG General Contracting. When the land was transferred to a Serpent River company, the City said RSG would remain the developer, builder and property manager.</p>

  <p>McLeod presented the Elliot Lake opportunity to Serpent River in May 2024. Serpent River members questioned the land-purchase terms and mortgage-payment structure. Council approved the project in July after reviewing their feedback and consulting the City.</p>

  <p>A groundbreaking was held on August 25, 2025. The project was supposed to begin producing occupied units in early 2026 and be finished that fall.</p>

  <h3>Both projects placed under review</h3>

  <p>By January 22, 2026, Elliot Lake had reached lot development but no building construction had begun. Serpent River's newly elected Council ordered a professional business evaluation of both urban housing projects.</p>

  <p>The minutes report a $13.5-million BMO line of credit supporting both developments, with $9.98 million drawn. Additional money was coming from Serpent River's Treaty account while long-term financing was arranged. Council recorded concerns about whether the projects remained feasible and prudent.</p>

  <p>A February update put the Elliot Lake construction estimate at about $10 million, with about $989,000 already spent. The options included continuing, selling, or stopping construction before selling or leasing.</p>

  <p>The public minutes reviewed for this article do not record the evaluator's final conclusion.</p>

  <p>The link back to Sagamok is direct: Guindon's 1000602533 Ontario Inc., the company that began the Elliot Lake transaction, is the company that sold South Market into Sagamok's project.</p>

  <h2>A GR Truss financing record connects the companies</h2>

  <p>On January 14, 2025, companies tied to McLeod and Guindon registered security over a John Deere payloader belonging to GR Truss.</p>

  <p>The Ontario filing names two secured parties:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>1000492658 Ontario Inc., the company operating as McLeod Consulting; and</li>
    <li>1000602533 Ontario Inc., Guindon's company that sold Sagamok the South Market land.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>In plain terms, both companies were listed as creditors with a security interest in the same machine.</p>

  <p>The registration predates Sagamok's April 1, 2025 acquisition of GR Truss by about eleven weeks. It does not state the amount, current balance or reason for the shared security. The underlying security agreement would explain the transaction.</p>

  <p>Sagamok paid a stated $100 for all of GR Truss's shares through its real-estate corporate structure. Buying the shares did not remove the company's existing debts or security registrations. Other public filings show that GR Truss already carried lender and equipment-finance obligations.</p>

  <p>After the acquisition, Sagamok Council authorized $900,000 for GR Truss through four cashflow advances and equity contributions. The first $500,000 advance was repayable at four per cent by March 2026. Its repayment status has not been posted.</p>

  <p>GR Truss has three directors: Chief Angus Toulouse, Councillor Michael Abitong and Councillor Norma Petahtegoose. Toulouse is President.</p>

  <p>The registry dates their roles to April 1, 2025. The posted Council record did not approve the board appointments until May 27, almost eight weeks later. The minutes record the motion as carried but do not record individual votes, disclosures or recusals.</p>

  <p>McLeod was advising Sagamok while his separate company remained listed as a secured creditor over a GR Truss asset after Sagamok acquired the business.</p>

  <p>The public record does not include McLeod Consulting's engagement terms, the payloader security agreement, or a record showing that Sagamok was informed of and approved the creditor position.</p>

  <h2>The documents that would answer the questions</h2>

  <p>Sagamok members should not have to reconstruct a $45-million housing project and an enterprise acquisition from land titles, corporate registries and another Nation's minutes.</p>

  <p>Four sets of records would answer the questions:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>The South Market purchase:</strong> the appraisal, purchase agreement, due-diligence report, development agreement, construction contract and current contractor.</li>
    <li><strong>The project financing:</strong> a reconciliation of the $300,000 and $7-million allocations, municipal incentives and proposed $38-million financing, together with the final lender, loan terms, guarantees and application status.</li>
    <li><strong>McLeod's roles:</strong> McLeod Consulting's engagement and compensation terms, the payloader security agreement, the amount and present balance, and the disclosure and approval record.</li>
    <li><strong>The GR Truss acquisition:</strong> the share-purchase agreement, closing balance sheet, debt schedule, repayment ledger, current financial statements and board minutes.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Serpent River named the companies, recorded the financing exposure, documented member concerns and disclosed that its incoming Council had ordered an independent review.</p>

  <p>Sagamok has not released the appraisal, purchase agreement, financing terms or security agreement that would explain its own transactions.</p>
